{
  "image_name": "397c059fcb08fc09f1efb26a813c9d13.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares four types of water electrolyzers and highlights how ions and water move between cathode and anode to produce hydrogen and oxygen. In all cases, hydrogen is released at the cathode and oxygen at the anode, but the electrolyte and charge carrier differ.\n\nThe first two panels show alkaline systems. In conventional alkaline water electrolysis, both electrodes sit in liquid KOH, and hydroxide ions travel through a separator from cathode to anode. In the anion-exchange membrane version, the liquid electrolyte is replaced by a solid membrane that still conducts OH⁻, giving a more compact layered cell.\n\nThe third panel shows proton-exchange membrane electrolysis, where water is fed on the anode side and protons move through the membrane toward the cathode. This reverses the ion direction compared with the alkaline cases.\n\nThe last panel presents high-temperature electrolysis, which uses steam rather than liquid water. Here oxide ions appear to move through a solid electrolyte from cathode to anode.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes that electrolyzer designs mainly differ in operating medium, membrane/electrolyte type, temperature, and which ionic species—OH⁻, H⁺, or O²⁻—carries charge across the cell.",
